# The layers that deny having a remainder at all. One row per layer filing the second branch of
# the `StatedRemainder` wrapper: a typed reason there is no remainder, and the note that argues for
# it.
#
# The model contradicted in its own format
#   This is what the wrapper is for. The model claims every layer has a remainder. Requiring the
#   wrapper forces a sender who disagrees to say so explicitly rather than leaving a field empty.
#   Every row here is somebody exercising that right.
#
# Why this arm is a `ClaimAbsence`
#   The right is narrower than it looks. "There is no remainder" reads two ways: nothing to
#   subtract from, or the difference is zero. Only the first is an absence. A difference of zero
#   is a clearance (`Fit` settles the line-to-line case in prose, "minimum clearance is zero"),
#   carrying a quantity of [0, 0, 0] and a sign, and an absence throws the sign away. So `none` is
#   not spellable here, and a layer that means "the two coincide exactly" files the clearance.
#
# Why the note is carried
#   When `ingest.sql` read only the first branch, a denial arrived as five NULLs (sign, absorber
#   and quantity all blank), which is exactly the shape of a document that said nothing. One of
#   the notes says so in as many words: "filed as a counter-example ... not as a gap in this
#   document". A gap is what that reading stores. The note is carried here because keeping only
#   the reason keeps the fact and loses the argument.
#
# Run this alone and read the notes
#   They say the same thing in different words: this layer states no nameplate, so `r = n - d`
#   has no `n` and the question is malformed rather than answered with nothing. Neither may say
#   "a supply with no quantum divides exactly", which is a different claim and a false one: no
#   quantum kills the residue term of `r = m*q - (demand mod q)` (layers/decomposed.sqlc) and
#   leaves `n - d` standing. Let
#   the notes disagree with the structure and the rule downstream prints the right reason ("no
#   nameplate is stated") beside the wrong one.
-- pm:Layer/pm:remainder taking the pm:absent branch of pm:StatedRemainder.
SELECT l.filing, l.layer,
       l.remainder_absent      AS reason,
       l.remainder_absent_note AS argument
FROM pm.layer l
WHERE l.remainder_absent IS NOT NULL
